95\",\"status\":\"DISCONNECTED\",\"slug\":\"outlook_contacts\",\"iconAsset\":\"connectorMicrosoftContacts\",\"connectedAccount\":null},{\"integrationId\":\"621332870257820\",\"status\":\"DISCONNECTED\",\"slug\":\"outlook_email\",\"iconAsset\":\"connectorMicrosoftOutlook\",\"connectedAccount\":null}],\"id\":\"viewer:1c19e211-7fef-449a-9858-f98cdca9c971\"}}}\n"])</script><link rel="preload" as="image" href="https://static.xx.fbcdn.net/assets/?set=hatch_connector_logos&amp;name=connectorGoogleCal&amp;density=3x" data-aria-hidden="true" aria-hidden="true"><link rel="preload" as="image" href="https://static.xx.fbcdn.net/assets/?set=hatch_connector_logos&amp;name=connectorGoogleGmail&amp;density=3x" data-aria-hidden="true" aria-hidden="true"><script nonce="">$RS("S:c","P:c")</script><script nonce="">$RC("B:a","S:a")</script><script nonce="">$RC("B:d","S:d")</script><script nonce="">$RC("B:4","S:4")</script><script nonce="">self.__next_f.push([1,"108:[{\"id\":\"2522364098269569\",\"text\":\"Compare clean energy types\",\"prompt\":\"Walk me through solar, wind, hydro, nuclear, and geothermal energy sources. Fire up subagents to explain how it actually converts its source into electricity. Generate a high-level infographic and some charts.\",\"iconName\":\"LIST_BADGE_SEARCH\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://scontent-sin11-2.xx.fbcdn.net/m1/v/t0.83350-7/AQPOe-B41h8kisyy8d6xvFt3wloTWP92CNmV99YFlJGOpNGlYDr0wMcItf7zMYkD-jip8Jig-wOg8wM0wVkjEJnRjuC0OU1krsW99VOuxf9Yr7a9Rr-bTK8e7Lhm5FzR1uJGxV2hvqNzdGnLsg.jpg?stp=s256x256\u0026_nc_gid=7m1bdfkOrAp75OGHOsZzag\u0026_nc_oc=AdrDX1H_sfZzmAfEisdySnHDXahdk2UlWhTBR99MJS_D04NdQ_PAOuMgvdETl5KhGOg\u0026strext=1\u0026ccb=10-5\u0026oh=00_AQSsfkT9QjiQGogEo5PFMxso-WBt9cL3ki7Czo9p9GLxCA\u0026oe=6AF3381E\u0026_nc_sid=087463\",\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"1987641768554253\",\"text\":\"Explain sci-fi cars with real physics\",\"prompt\":\"Walk me through the DeLorean time machine, the Magic School Bus, a Star Wars X-Wing, Mario Kart, and the Flintstones' foot-car. Fire up sub-agents to explain what physics/engineering would actually be needed to make each one work.\",\"iconName\":\"LIST_BADGE_SEARCH\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"1553254852933005\",\"text\":\"Turn notes into a study guide\",\"prompt\":\"Here's a picture of my handwritten notes. Transcribe them and create an interactive artifact organized by topic with quizzes to test my understanding.\",\"iconName\":\"BOOK_OPEN\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://scontent-sin11-2.xx.fbcdn.net/m1/v/t0.83350-7/AQOIj40GpU1KRqornUlYDyZWzRargFkpAJ3rHjCCM0U2N-1KN9owMtjC4zJrAMgsMKc6tI4Pn06s4XWqRaJlI2-w8ZUcWVT7PCVnKJsWTIubbsvXaxZArdeKAqQawhkOCbRLKwVuJBLUZfT1Fw.jpg?stp=s256x256\u0026_nc_gid=7m1bdfkOrAp75OGHOsZzag\u0026_nc_oc=AdpFqXtGyPOQgqpnhjRf1F9dPTbSqu1RLxfikysve6uIKCuIPVvkIyyyMMAFLx63KuI\u0026strext=1\u0026ccb=10-5\u0026oh=00_AQRMtzjyHIkM4dwK04EVcQsoJDeRAXezZuzEyOlikDJkNg\u0026oe=6AF33A90\u0026_nc_sid=087463\",\"attachmentType\":\"MEDIA_PICKER\",\"mode\":\"think_hard\"},{\"id\":\"1057641619954637\",\"text\":\"Explain mythical creature biology\",\"prompt\":\"Walk me through dragons, phoenixes, krakens, unicorns, and mermaids. Fire up sub-agents to explain how each creature's signature ability would actually work biologically (fire breathing, regeneration, deep-sea pressure survival, etc.). Organize response by creature and include an image or infographic of each.\",\"iconName\":\"BOOK\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_hard\"},{\"id\":\"2929033954104297\",\"text\":\"Plan a perfect Saturday\",\"prompt\":\"I have a free Saturday coming up. Fire up 3 sub agents \u0026 give me 3 options: something outdoorsy, something cultural, and something lazy — all near me. Check what's trending and happening in my area this weekend. Include in line content to show me what my options are.\",\"iconName\":\"SUN_HAZE\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://scontent-sin11-2.xx.fbcdn.net/m1/v/t0.83350-7/AQOeBE0UKt0Tv2IlwrGWhYeHMXuW631PwnYrJbx47CkGrtosYL156fmNMuFhtXYkLpGZ88kxPm-n8Qc9WNZcBaR5iRmjHnbewvXzXBTd8wrEzt1XCBr1oALuYeANz9OLc0P0wNW3lmDDIGzGsA.jpg?stp=s256x256\u0026_nc_gid=7m1bdfkOrAp75OGHOsZzag\u0026_nc_oc=AdocpswX4Cb3PgYZ_GWQl0zaDnVZ7IgM6sx6Lr_2pYpR26jUlKoQZnrrL8eXIy1R-j0\u0026strext=1\u0026ccb=10-5\u0026oh=00_AQQjajT9J0jLa3wVCdgpYvaQqNxoX7iZFziKPzTnL39unQ\u0026oe=6AF32F2B\u0026_nc_sid=087463\",\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"25952502591114573\",\"text\":\"Plan a Korean BBQ dinner\",\"prompt\":\"Make a rich and engaging artifact to walk me through how to prepare a traditional korean bbq dinner at home\",\"iconName\":\"FORK_KNIFE\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://scontent-sin11-2.xx.fbcdn.net/m1/v/t0.83350-7/AQNyCi5VkutLruhx24oTpfEY9U3tzZycRpaSP8gOhvAPGlYgwthXDy__L287hWpujXRrcRRV_EEFUL3_oFde2Vjr0cTvij1kpPuFE_F5PVe3UFCD_BtgZgzJrteuLbgfVOCyEKfxDHGXFECYlA.jpg?stp=s256x256\u0026_nc_gid=7m1bdfkOrAp75OGHOsZzag\u0026_nc_oc=Adpc4Q9MqJ02hKwK8BUemFuqoDmOkO6Z78zd74QE1uSyO--owDKWjXLqd_8v3eFAtZw\u0026strext=1\u0026ccb=10-5\u0026oh=00_AQQC7B-nHxaQ7SH9qX29-vJdFUEgBixVr9Eq-IW6njD4Tg\u0026oe=6AF34A64\u0026_nc_sid=087463\",\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_hard\"},{\"id\":\"27859575287011134\",\"text\":\"Create a run plan for beginners\",\"prompt\":\"I want to start running but I have no experience. Use subagents to give me a couch-to-5K plan and tips to not quit after week 1.\",\"iconName\":\"FIGURE_RUNNING\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://scontent-sin11-2.xx.fbcdn.net/m1/v/t0.83350-7/AQPCGxUhDzGiKUgOcs9AR6yEKqBZVc-mw8aM143Q3PKMzH9-T9Ora-_fS_ZQCYiMGU10jyu8PucSk5dLeI8Ci4_GvMwDNhZ85xuGj6PkyjOUqvuGSX48_75srhmbzcJr5380dFzblKFXEzVt8w.jpg?stp=s256x256\u0026_nc_gid=7m1bdfkOrAp75OGHOsZzag\u0026_nc_oc=AdpHFuLudoJTu1lNO9yCmHohFq94-6w9F_XKOVEZUMoGMy1RuV_wEmY_BzJTZnpu7R4\u0026strext=1\u0026ccb=10-5\u0026oh=00_AQQpyLHstaAFXLumuXsS1naYKKlHHQwFEKEGYMG7Knxl2Q\u0026oe=6AF33A80\u0026_nc_sid=087463\",\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"37298981036384422\",\"text\":\"Make a trending seasonal recipe\",\"prompt\":\"Find me a trending recipe using seasonal and local ingredients. Put the recipe into an artifact that is engaging and interactive with offered substitutions and media.\",\"iconName\":\"WHISK_SPATULA\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://scontent-sin11-2.xx.fbcdn.net/m1/v/t0.83350-7/AQOG8SnUkC3xYne192WUb5dfbJooTAjxu-g4NxY7D9OcX6QuiUXMTqDE9bjuhecby37bYdZDvDSDXRPY15LKldV7zNF44xQULTlFTFpg5P_fa_vbmRG2ShhUK9WJFIQbTfUGp6uBKt38z3iO.jpg?stp=s256x256\u0026_nc_gid=7m1bdfkOrAp75OGHOsZzag\u0026_nc_oc=Adpyj6F4RUqLU78sUxJZq3tiDhhIFZJySa7CJX0KSNbd4rlqFkGV7OZj-cxdyJlb564\u0026strext=1\u0026ccb=10-5\u0026oh=00_AQTmI52pifL0K0aUHzT9mZhCduLe4lpd6--luonmq9zU9A\u0026oe=6AF33666\u0026_nc_sid=087463\",\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_hard\"}]\n109:[{\"id\":\"918225390774789\",\"text\":\"Play couples court\",\"prompt\":\"Run a courtroom-style hearing on a silly everyday argument. Present the plaintiff's case, then the defendant's. Cross-examine both sides. Deliver a dramatic verdict with a gavel. Pick from disputes like: Is it rude to not text back for 24 hours? Should the toilet seat stay up or down? Can you eat your roommate's clearly-labeled leftovers if they've been there 5 days? Go.\",\"iconName\":\"LIST_BADGE_SEARCH\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"3398001763702881\",\"text\":\"Get this week's forecast\",\"prompt\":\"Give me a weather forecast for the next 7 days\",\"iconName\":\"SUN_HAZE\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"954852147623880\",\"text\":\"Explain what a data center is\",\"prompt\":\"\\\"Explain what data centers are, what they're used for, why they matter, and how they connect to the apps and services I use every day. Keep it simple and focus on the big picture. \\\"\",\"iconName\":\"BOOK\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"916620940811833\",\"text\":\"Recap last night's big game\",\"prompt\":\"Tell me the highlights, key moments, and final score from whatever major sports game happened yesterday\",\"iconName\":\"BUILDING_STADIUM\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"1564465362012181\",\"text\":\"Learn a new language\",\"prompt\":\"I want to learn a new language. Ask me follow up questions to tailor a plan.\",\"iconName\":\"TRANSLATE\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":null},{\"id\":\"1387465133483328\",\"text\":\"Check my homework\",\"prompt\":\"Can you help me check my homework? I don't just want the answers, I want to understand how I can improve.\",\"iconName\":\"GRADUATION_CAP\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"MEDIA_PICKER\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"1033771995697152\",\"text\":\"Learn an unbelievable fact\",\"prompt\":\"Give me an absurd and unexpected fact\",\"iconName\":\"BOOK_OPEN\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_fast\"},{\"id\":\"1359066512346911\",\"text\":\"Find easy 20 minute dinners\",\"prompt\":\"Find 3 easy 20-minute dinners based on what's trending on Instagram - one veg, one chicken, one pasta\",\"iconName\":\"SALAD\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"attachmentType\":\"NONE\",\"mode\":\"think_hard\"}]\n10a:[\"$108:6\",\"$108:1\",\"$109:0\",\"$108:3\",\"$109:2\",\"$108:5\"]\n"])</script><script nonce="">self.__next_f.push([1,"104:{\"data\":{\"conversationStartersByType\":[{\"id\":\"1658695762220852\",\"text\":\"Create action items from notes\",\"longPrompt\":\"Create action items from notes\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"INFO_GATHERING\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"default\"},{\"id\":\"1260708592793455\",\"text\":\"Describe what's happening in an image\",\"longPrompt\":\"I'll share an image — tell me what you see and what's going on in it.\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"CAMERA_MEDIA\",\"category\":\"MULTIMODAL\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"},{\"id\":\"1995013938110921\",\"text\":\"Transcribe handwritten notes\",\"longPrompt\":\"Transcribe handwritten notes\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"CAMERA_MEDIA\",\"category\":\"INFO_GATHERING\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"default\"},{\"id\":\"909839255192169\",\"text\":\"Highlight insights from a report\",\"longPrompt\":\"I have a report with a lot of data in it and want to understand what it's really saying. I'll share it — help me pull out the most important takeaways.\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"MEDIA_PICKER\",\"category\":\"IDEAS\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"}]}}\n"])</script><script nonce="">$RC("B:14","S:14")</script><script nonce="">self.__next_f.push([1,"103:{\"data\":{\"conversationStartersByType\":[{\"id\":\"2068324373733161\",\"text\":\"Discover home decor trends\",\"longPrompt\":\"What are the home decor trends that are blowing up on Instagram\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"TRENDING\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"},{\"id\":\"969760712134847\",\"text\":\"Learn a new language\",\"longPrompt\":\"I want to learn a new language. Ask me follow up questions to tailor a plan.\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"SYSTEM_PROMPT_MODES\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"},{\"id\":\"1348266374002738\",\"text\":\"Critique my resume\",\"longPrompt\":\"Can you take an honest look at my resume and tell me what's working and what isn't?\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"WRITE\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"},{\"id\":\"3067400220130591\",\"text\":\"Discover trending restaurants near me\",\"longPrompt\":\"Recommend trending restaurants near me\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"NEWS\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"}]}}\n"])</script><script nonce="">$RC("B:13","S:13")</script><script nonce="">self.__next_f.push([1,"100:{\"data\":{\"conversationStartersByType\":[{\"id\":\"983509644111893\",\"text\":\"Uncover under the radar celebrity couples\",\"longPrompt\":\"Uncover under the radar celebrity couples\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"SUPPORT\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"},{\"id\":\"1433117561847204\",\"text\":\"Debug my code\",\"longPrompt\":\"My code isn't working and I can't figure out why. I'll share it — help me find the bug and explain what went wrong.\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"TEST\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"},{\"id\":\"3067400220130591\",\"text\":\"Discover trending restaurants near me\",\"longPrompt\":\"Recommend trending restaurants near me\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"NEWS\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"},{\"id\":\"1638220524033682\",\"text\":\"Show me the most iconic Coachella sets of all time\",\"longPrompt\":\"Search Instagram for the most iconic and talked-about Coachella sets of all time\",\"type\":\"learn\",\"attachment_type\":\"NONE\",\"category\":\"TRENDING\",\"responseDisplayMode\":\"full_prompt\"}]}}\n"])</script><script nonce="">$RC("B:10","S:10")</script><script nonce="">self.__next_f.push([1,"102:{\"data\":{\"conversationStartersByType\":[]}}\n"])</script><script nonce="">$RC("B:12","S:12")</script><script nonce="">self.__next_f.push([1,"10e:{\"data\":{\"notifications_count\":0}}\n"])</script><script nonce="">$RC("B:17","S:17")</script><script nonce="">self.__next_f.push([1,"113:T7d8,Search the web for weather and fashion trends in my town/city. If my town/city is unavailable, use the most populous city in my country or region. Search the shopping catalogue for clothing items and style me using the exact clothing to create an outfit that matches the local weather and fashion trends. Recommend shopping items in the outfit for me to purchase, show shopping recommendations after text. Use my likeness, with my face, hair and skin tone unchanged and clearly recognisable. If my likeness is unavailable, use a neutral mannequin.\nCreate an image of me standing in an easy contrapposto with the weight carried on one leg and the opposite hip dropped, shoulders soft — the stance in which a piece of clothing hangs the way it hangs in life. Bright high-key fashion e-commerce photography in the modern Japanese minimal style; seamless studio background in EXACTLY the warm oat off-white #EAE4DA — not white, not grey, and never cool or blue toned; the backdrop colour is #EAE4DA edge to edge with no gradient and no colour cast; very soft near-shadowless daylight-balanced lighting from a large overhead softbox with broad fill on both sides; clean neutral colour, gentle contrast, no heavy shadow anywhere; the model relaxed and natural with an easy, neutral expression, approachable rather than aloof; full-length photograph with the feet in frame, shot on an 85-mm lens at f/5.6 from eye level; the framing, the distance to the subject and the lighting are IDENTICAL in every frame of this set, and the backdrop is the same exact #EAE4DA warm oat in every frame with no shift in hue or brightness between them, so that ONLY THE POSE CHANGES. Photorealistic fashion photography, shot on a full-frame camera, natural skin texture and natural hair, anatomically correct hands, the piece of clothing fitting correctly with believable fabric weight and drape. No text, no watermark, no logo overlay, no collage, no split frame, a single subject only. Send the image before the message text.114:T7d8,Search the web for weather and fashion trends in my town/city. If my town/city is unavailable, use the most populous city in my country or region. Search the shopping catalogue for clothing items and style me using the exact clothing to create an outfit that matches the local weather and fashion trends. Recommend shopping items in the outfit for me to purchase, show shopping recommendations after text. Use my likeness, with my face, hair and skin tone unchanged and clearly recognisable. If my likeness is unavailable, use a neutral mannequin.\nCreate an image of me standing in an easy contrapposto with the weight carried on one leg and the opposite hip dropped, shoulders soft — the stance in which a piece of clothing hangs the way it hangs in life. Bright high-key fashion e-commerce photography in the modern Japanese minimal style; seamless studio background in EXACTLY the warm oat off-white #EAE4DA — not white, not grey, and never cool or blue toned; the backdrop colour is #EAE4DA edge to edge with no gradient and no colour cast; very soft near-shadowless daylight-balanced lighting from a large overhead softbox with broad fill on both sides; clean neutral colour, gentle contrast, no heavy shadow anywhere; the model relaxed and natural with an easy, neutral expression, approachable rather than aloof; full-length photograph with the feet in frame, shot on an 85-mm lens at f/5.6 from eye level; the framing, the distance to the subject and the lighting are IDENTICAL in every frame of this set, and the backdrop is the same exact #EAE4DA warm oat in every frame with no shift in hue or brightness between them, so that ONLY THE POSE CHANGES. Photorealistic fashion photography, shot on a full-frame camera, natural skin texture and natural hair, anatomically correct hands, the piece of clothing fitting correctly with believable fabric weight and drape. No text, no watermark, no logo overlay, no collage, no split frame, a single subject only. Send the image before the message text.115:T6513,Create four personalized fighter stickers using the three supplied images.\n\nInputs\n\n  Input 1: User identity photo\n    Supplies identity, face, skin tone, age, hair, facial hair, accessories.\n\n  Input 2: Exact outfit reference\n    Supplies complete shirt, shorts, gloves with logos, patterns, materials, colors, placement. When creating the reference image, do not give the user shoes. They should not be given shoes.\n\n  Input 3: Typography sheet\n    Transparent image with four cells: top left GOAT, top right LFG, bottom left AND NEW, bottom right UNDISPUTED. This is actual artwork, not inspiration. If uploaded with white background, convert white to transparent and remove, preserving text shape. Do not fill transparent areas with color.\n\nSegmentation background rule (applies everywhere)\n\n  Use ONLY pure flat uniform green #00FF00 (RGB 0, 255, 0) for all generated backgrounds. This green is for segmentation only and will be removed to transparency later. Do not use magenta #FF00FF, pink, purple, or any other background color. No gradient, texture, shadows, or vignette in the background.\n\nText artwork source rule (applies to every generation and edit)\n\n  The only allowed lettering source is the original prepared typography crop image.\n  Every image generation or image edit that shows lettering must have {{word artwork}} attached as an image input.\n  A filename, phrase, written description, or previously generated sticker is never a substitute.\n  Do not redraw, retype, trace, substitute, approximate, or reinterpret the lettering.\n  Keep the lettering in front of the fighter, at its original size and position.\n  This applies to initial creation, corrections, retries, and all follow-up turns.\n\nStage 1: Canonical fighter reference\n\n  Use Input 1 + Input 2 to generate one isolated full-body reference of the person wearing the supplied outfit.\n\n  - Preserve recognizable facial structure, skin tone, age, hair, facial hair, hat/glasses/religious coverings.\n  - Glasses, hats, religious coverings, hearing devices, and any other visible face/head accessories in Input 1 are part of the person's identity. Preserve them in the canonical fighter reference and all four stickers, with the same design, colors, and way they are worn. Do not remove, replace, or redesign them for a pose or expression.\n  - Natural photographic anatomy, no exaggerated muscles, no enlarged head/hands/feet.\n  - Transfer exact shirt, shorts, gloves: cuts, lengths, colors, materials, patterns, logos, placement. Natural folds allowed. Do not simplify or invent marks.\n  - Bare feet unless footwear in Input 2.\n  - Photorealistic, slightly stronger contrast, moody directional shadows. Editorial grade, not illustration.\n  - One person, neutral standing pose, entire hat/hair, both hands, both legs, both feet visible, comfortable margins.\n  - Generate on flat uniform green #00FF00, no shadows or gradient. No border. No magenta. Retain as canonical fighter reference. Do not present to user.\n  - Do not give the user shoes.\n\n  Before proceeding, confirm that the reference retains the face/head accessories visible in Input 1 and that the background is pure #00FF00.\n\nStage 2: Split typography\n\n  Read Input 3 dimensions, crop at horizontal and vertical midpoints into:\n    text_goat (top-left), text_lfg (top-right), text_and_new (bottom-left), text_undisputed (bottom-right).\n\n  Preserve full canvas, placement, alpha. Do not tightly crop lettering. Keep each cell's original dimensions and preserve the text's exact size and coordinates relative to that cell. Top stays top, middle stays middle, bottom stays bottom; horizontal placement also stays unchanged. Do not move, resize, recenter, stretch, or reflow the words. Do not extend the cell canvas. Use these unchanged crops as {{word artwork}} for generation and every subsequent edit. Do not fill transparent areas with green or magenta.\n\nStage 3: Four parallel image edits (border in-generation)\n\n  Run one complete image-edit per cell in parallel. Each task gets: its prepared typography crop as base, the canonical fighter reference, and Input 2 as support. Do not pass only text descriptions.\n\n  Use the same sub-agent prompt below for all four tasks. Substitute only {{word}}, {{word artwork}}, {{pose}}, and {{output file}} using the definitions after the template. Replace {{pose}} with the complete pose description, not a pose number or summary. Resolve {{word artwork}} to the actual prepared image and attach it; its name alone is not an image input.\n\n  Do not paraphrase, shorten, embellish, or add different instructions for different agents. The only per-agent differences are those four substitutions and the corresponding typography crop. Every agent receives the same canonical reference and outfit image.\n\n  Include the same border description and complete all-pixel background-removal instructions in every agent task, including corrections. Define one shared deterministic cleanup routine and make all agents reuse it; do not have each agent independently invent its segmentation algorithm. Keep image generation parallel. If agents cannot share the routine, collect their untouched renders and run that same cleanup routine on all four centrally.\n\n  Follow-up routing\n\n    The follow-up edits and retries rule inside the sub-agent template is mandatory for the parent and every sub-agent on this turn and all follow-up turns. Keep each sticker associated with its original prepared typography crop. Apply the rule to every correction, including edits unrelated to lettering. For follow-up jobs, use its correction template rather than inventing a new prompt. Run independent corrections to affected stickers in parallel where supported; leave unaffected stickers unchanged.\n\nSub-agent prompt template\n\n  Begin sub-agent prompt\n\n    Create the {{word}} sticker and return {{output file}}.\n\n    Segmentation background: Use ONLY pure flat uniform green #00FF00. Do not use magenta #FF00FF or any other color. No gradient, texture, or shadows in the background.\n\n    Text source: {{word artwork}} is the only lettering source and must be attached. Do not retype the words.\n\n    You have three attached images in this order:\n      1. {{word artwork}}: the prepared typography crop. This is the base image to edit, not a style reference or inspiration.\n      2. Canonical fighter reference: the person, proportions, outfit, and photographic treatment.\n      3. Input 2: the exact outfit reference, supplied as support.\n\n    Pass these actual images to the image-edit tool in the same order. Do not send only the fighter reference or replace the typography crop with a written description.\n\n    In one image-edit generation, add the fighter and white border while retaining the supplied typography artwork unchanged as a single integrated image. Do not generate standalone pose and paste. Do not use Python to composite.\n\n    Send the following image-edit prompt verbatim to the image tool. Do not rewrite it or invent a new generation prompt.\n\n    Begin image-edit prompt\n\n      Edit the attached {{word artwork}} base image to create the {{word}} sticker as a single integrated image.\n\n      Background first: Pure flat uniform green #00FF00 only. No magenta, no pink, no purple. No gradient, texture, or shadows in the background. This green will be keyed to transparency later.\n\n      Typography lock: preserve the supplied artwork\n\n      The lettering in {{word artwork}} is finished, pre-rendered artwork. It is NOT a font suggestion, style reference, or request to generate new text. {{word}} identifies the supplied phrase; it is not an instruction to typeset it again.\n\n      - Use the actual supplied lettering as fixed source artwork. Preserve its original pixels and appearance; add the fighter behind it in the same generation so the result looks integrated, rather than recreating the words.\n      - Preserve the exact font representation: every letter shape, stroke weight, corner, angle, slant, spacing, line arrangement, curvature, scale, and position.\n      - The crop determines placement: keep the text at its exact original size and location within this cell, top, middle, bottom, left, or right as supplied. Position the fighter around that fixed placement; do not move or resize the text to accommodate the pose.\n      - Preserve the exact color, material, texture, bevel, extrusion, highlights, shadows, and existing edge treatment. Do not make the lettering more glossy, rounded, dimensional, flat, distressed, or otherwise different from the source.\n      - Do not redraw, retype, trace, substitute, approximate, or reinterpret the typography. Correct spelling in a similar-looking font is NOT an acceptable match.\n      - Keep the original lettering in the foreground, in front of the fighter. Do not obscure, omit, or duplicate any letters.\n      - Apply the fighter's photographic lighting and color treatment to the fighter only, not to the supplied lettering. Integration means natural placement behind the text, not restyling the text.\n      - The requested white sticker border may sit outside and behind the existing artwork. It must not paint over the lettering, thicken its strokes, or replace its existing edge treatment.\n      - Keep the supplied lettering unchanged through generation, background removal, and export. Preserve its legitimate highlights and details during cleanup. Always use the attached {{word artwork}} image as the source, never a description.\n\n      Assigned pose: {{pose}}\n\n      Shared composition:\n      - Supplied typography as foreground, in front of fighter, unchanged: words, spelling, letterforms, color, highlights, size, spacing, position. Do not recreate, duplicate, or add text.\n      - Fighter large behind type, full-length head-to-toe, entire pose inside canvas: hat/hair, raised gloves, knees, ankles, both feet inside the image edges. Keep the text in front at its original location in {{word artwork}}.\n      - Aim for pose to occupy 84-88% of canvas height. Pull camera back to fit, never distort anatomy, never crop hat/gloves/feet.\n      - Only fighter + supplied typography in artwork on green background. No splash graphics, shadows across canvas, or extra text.\n\n      Consistency: same canonical fighter, same outfit, same photographic treatment. Only pose/expression change. No punching/kicking/opponents/weapons. All gestures are empty-handed: no held objects or props.\n\n      Accessory lock: Preserve all glasses, hats, religious coverings, hearing devices, and other face/head accessories from the canonical fighter reference. They are part of identity and must stay on, with the same design, colors, and way they are worn. Do not remove, replace, or redesign them. Pose directions about hair or energy must not override this accessory lock.\n\n      Add a full-length, head-to-toe photorealistic fighter behind the supplied foreground lettering as one integrated image. Use the attached canonical reference for person, proportions, outfit, and photographic treatment. Fit entire pose inside canvas, keeping the supplied lettering at its original size and position in front of the fighter. Render as a die-cut sticker. Use one clean, slim but clearly visible solid-white outline, with enough weight to read as a die-cut sticker edge, not a wispy hairline, thick band, or padded backing. Keep its apparent thickness even around the artwork and consistent across the pack. Place it tightly around the combined outer silhouette of fighter + lettering, behind the artwork, with a smooth antialiased outer edge. No wide white margins, nested outlines, glow, or shadow. Keep genuine open gaps between the figure and lettering open rather than filling them with a white backing. Keep the supplied typography artwork unchanged while generating the fighter and white border on a perfectly flat uniform green #00FF00 background, no gradient, texture, or shadows, no magenta.\n\n    End image-edit prompt\n\n    Follow-up edits and retries: original text image required every time\n\n    Whenever you edit a sticker output, whether for a correction, retry, or later user request, you MUST supply its respective original typography crop as an additional image in that image-edit call. This applies even when the requested change concerns only the person, pose, outfit, accessories, border, or background. Every final image with text must be generated from the cutout.\n\n    For follow-up edits, use this image order instead of the initial-generation order:\n\n      1. {{edit target}}: the current sticker output being corrected; this is the base image to edit.\n      2. {{word artwork}}: the corresponding original prepared typography crop from Input 3; this is the authoritative lettering artwork.\n      3. Canonical fighter reference, plus Input 2 when needed for the correction.\n\n    The actual typography image must be attached to EVERY image-edit call that shows lettering. A filename, phrase, written font description, earlier attachment in the conversation, or text extracted from a generated sticker is not a substitute. Never treat an already altered output as the typography source.\n\n    If the original crop is unavailable, recover it from Input 3 with the same crop and canvas preparation. If neither source is available, ask for the source image again rather than guessing or retyping the lettering.\n\n    For correction jobs, {{edit target}} is the actual current output image and {{requested change}} is the requested correction. {{word}} and {{word artwork}} keep their original sticker-specific bindings.\n\n    Send this correction image-edit prompt verbatim after substituting the variables:\n\n      Edit image 1, {{edit target}}, only to apply this correction: {{requested change}}. Use ONLY pure #00FF00 green for any background areas. Image 2, {{word artwork}}, is the original supplied lettering artwork for {{word}} and must be used as the exact typography source. It is not a font suggestion or style reference. Preserve its original lettering exactly: words, letterforms, stroke weights, slant, spacing, arrangement, curvature, scale, position, colors, texture, bevel, extrusion, highlights, shadows, and edge treatment. Keep that lettering in the foreground, in front of the fighter, at its exact original size and location within image 2. Do not redraw, retype, substitute, restyle, embellish, omit, or duplicate it. If image 1 already contains altered lettering, restore it to match image 2 rather than preserving the altered version. Use one clean, slim but clearly visible solid-white outline, with enough weight to read as a die-cut sticker edge, not a wispy hairline, thick band, or padded backing. Keep its apparent thickness even around the artwork and consistent across the pack. The white sticker border must remain outside and behind the lettering, not repaint or thicken its strokes. Change nothing else beyond the requested correction and any necessary restoration of the source typography. Follow the shared all-pixel background-removal and export requirements, including removal of enclosed green background. Do not introduce magenta.\n\n    After every edit, compare the returned lettering against the actual original {{word artwork}}, not just the previous output or the phrase. Check that the background is still pure #00FF00 with no magenta. Do not mark a stylistic substitution as passed. Reattach the original crop again on every further image-edit call.\n\n    Include this rule and correction template verbatim in any follow-up task you delegate. Deterministic background cleanup must also preserve the original lettering; if cleanup uses image generation, the same additional-image requirement applies.\n\n  End sub-agent prompt\n\nStage 4: Shared all-pixel background removal (no post border)\n\n  Remove flat green #00FF00 to transparency using one shared deterministic chroma-key routine. Do NOT add a white border in post. The border is already in the generation.\n\n  Background color, not border connectivity, determines removal\n\n  - Save the untouched generated image before keying. Always calculate the mask from that source, not from a damaged or repeatedly keyed export.\n  - Validate first: background must be pure #00FF00. If you see magenta #FF00FF, pink, or purple, treat as generation failure and regenerate, do not try to key magenta to transparency.\n  - Sample the actual key color from clear background near the canvas edges. Evaluate the resulting color test on EVERY pixel across the entire image, including enclosed gaps. Sampling at the edges does not restrict where removal applies.\n  - Use key-color hue and saturation together with clear green dominance over both red and blue, allowing ordinary variation around the generated background color. Do not use exact RGB equality, brightness, darkness, white content, or the green channel alone. Convert RGB values to floating point before subtraction or squaring.\n  - Apply the SAME calibrated color test to exterior and enclosed background. Do not require a connection to the canvas edge, a flood-fill path through the white border, a minimum component size, or a second manual seed for each pocket.\n  - A confirmed background-green pixel must become transparent whether it is outside the sticker, inside a letter counter, between limbs, or fully enclosed by the white outline.\n  - Protect actual foreground pixels, not an entire filled silhouette or bounding box. Being inside the outer sticker outline does not make a green gap part of the artwork.\n  - Preserve genuine green foreground details identified from the source references with specific protection for those details only. If a candidate green region is ambiguous, inspect it against the untouched render and references; do not protect every enclosed region or erase all greens indiscriminately.\n\n  Alpha and foreground preservation\n\n  - Build one color-based background mask for the whole canvas. Confirmed key-green background gets alpha zero. Solid non-background artwork keeps its original colors and opacity. Soft alpha is limited to genuine foreground/background transitions, including enclosed-gap boundaries.\n  - If the source already contains transparency, never make it opaque during keying. Combine source alpha and key alpha using their per-pixel minimum. Use the untouched source alpha, not the alpha from a damaged export.\n  - Do not run fill-holes, fill-contours, convex-hull filling, or an operation that resets everything inside the outer silhouette to opaque. These operations would put the enclosed background back into the sticker.\n  - Preserve skin, face, hair, clothing, gloves, logos, red lettering, and the generated white border. A letter opening showing the person's clothing or body is foreground, not background. Transparency in the original text crop alone does not determine transparency in the completed image.\n  - Keep solid foreground interiors fully opaque. Do not create pinholes or pockmarks in skin, clothing, lettering, or the white outline. If the key damages them, restore from the untouched source and correct the color test or specific protection, not by blanket filling all holes.\n  - Correct key-color contamination only along actual foreground/background transitions. Remove green from mixed edge colors while retaining antialiasing and the generated border's thickness. Do not replace green pockets with white, desaturate them to gray, expand the border, or blur them away. Do not introduce magenta.\n  - No global erosion, blanket blur, or aggressive threshold increase across the image to chase a single pocket. The fix is applying the correct key everywhere, not deleting more foreground.\n\n  Fast final alpha cleanup: same settings for all four\n\n  After keying, run one lightweight deterministic cleanup pass at the final export resolution, using the same routine and settings for all four outputs.\n\n  - In confirmed background residue only, set alpha values of 8 or less on a 0-255 scale to zero. Do not apply this threshold inside protected foreground, genuine fine detail, supplied typography, or its antialiased edges.\n  - Remove only tiny, fully detached background specks using 8-connected components: 1-4 pixels at a 1024-pixel shorter dimension, with the area cutoff scaled proportionally to image area at other resolutions. This cutoff is for detached residue, not a restriction on removing green background pockets. Never keep only the largest component; separate letters and intentional details must remain.\n  - Preserve normal partially transparent antialiased pixels along the real outline and genuine interior background boundaries. Do not make the entire alpha channel binary.\n  - Do not resize, recrop, restyle, regenerate, or add a new border.\n\n  Verify the exported file, not just the preview\n\n  Reload the exported PNG and check that every pixel classified as confirmed key-green background in the untouched source has alpha zero, including enclosed areas. Exclude only specifically verified genuine green foreground details. Any remaining opaque classified background means the mask or export failed; correct it before delivery. Do not hide a failed alpha mask by recoloring the green. Confirm no magenta pixels remain.\n\n  Compare the cleaned sticker with the untouched generation over dark and light backgrounds. Confirm no solid foreground detail has become transparent and no green pockets, magenta pixels, detached specks, or colored fringes remain. Keep the generated white border and original typography intact. Segmentation corrections use the untouched render and shared routine, not another image-generation request.\n\n  Final validation per sticker:\n\n    1. Face matches reference.\n    2. Outfit consistent.\n    3. Full pose inside canvas, hat/gloves/knees/feet not cut.\n    4. Background in generated source was pure #00FF00 only, no magenta, before keying.\n    5. Lettering in front of the fighter at its original size and location within the source cell.\n    6. Text intact, readable, appears once, and matches the supplied {{word artwork}} in font, letterforms, styling, color, effects, size, spacing, and position. Compare against the actual crop, not merely the phrase; a font substitution or stylistic reinterpretation must not be marked as passed.\n    7. Both exterior and enclosed green background fully removed and verified in the exported alpha, no fringe, and no new transparent holes or lost pixels inside solid foreground regions.\n    8. Generated white border slim but clearly visible, neither hairline-thin nor thick, even and consistent across the pack, clean, opaque, intact, not clipped.\n    9. Transparency only in background and genuine gaps, with smooth antialiasing at their boundaries.\n    10. All face/head accessories from the canonical reference are retained.\n    11. All poses are empty-handed; no actual bow, arrow, bowstring, or other prop appears.\n\n  Return {{output file}} as a transparent PNG. Preserve prepared canvas. Do not present reference or intermediates.\n\nVariable definitions: substitute these into the template\n\n  Goat, top-left:\n    word = GOAT\n    word artwork = text_goat\n    output file = GOAT.png\n    pose = Full front view, feet wide apart, legs straight with slight bend in the knees, weight evenly distributed. Both arms raised high and bent at 90 degrees, fists clenched and flexing biceps outward. Chest up, chin slightly up. Expression: mouth wide open in a triumphant yell, eyes squinted with intensity.\n\n  LFG, top-right:\n    word = LFG\n    word artwork = text_lfg\n    output file = LFG.png\n    pose = Full front view, wide athletic power stance, feet planted wider than shoulders, knees deeply bent, hips low, torso leaning slightly forward. Both arms bent and held low and wide at the sides, fists clenched tight at hip height. Chest expanded. Head tilted back slightly. Expression: mouth wide open in a primal roar, eyes squeezed shut or looking up. Should feel grounded, aggressive, and explosive.\n\n  And New, bottom-left:\n    word = AND NEW\n    word artwork = text_and_new\n    output file = AND_NEW.png\n    pose = Frontal 3/4 view, down on one knee, right knee on the ground, left leg forward with foot flat, knee bent at 90 degrees. Torso turned slightly to camera left. Left arm extended straight forward with a closed gloved fist. Right arm pulled back, elbow bent and high, closed gloved fist held near the chin. Head turned to look along the extended left arm. Expression: focused, stoic, lips pressed, intense stare. This is an empty-handed celebration gesture: both hands hold nothing. No bow, arrow, bowstring, equipment, or other prop.\n\n  Undisputed, bottom-right:\n    word = UNDISPUTED\n    word artwork = text_undisputed\n    output file = UNDISPUTED.png\n    pose = Three-quarter side view facing camera right, torso open slightly toward the camera rather than turned away. Stationary, both bare feet planted in a natural staggered stance. Head follows the torso with no look-back twist. Right elbow bent, right gloved index finger held vertically to the lips in a shush gesture. Left arm hangs naturally down beside the hip, with the left glove clearly visible and separated from the torso. Exactly two arms and two gloved hands; only the right hand is raised. Expression: calm, confident, subtle smirk.\n\nOutput\n\n  Four separate transparent PNGs in order: GOAT.png, LFG.png, AND_NEW.png, UNDISPUTED.png. Preserve prepared canvas. Do not combine into sheet. Do not present reference or intermediates.\n\nImportant\n\n  Parallelize when possible, and try to minimize the time to a users results without sacrificing quality. When presenting the sets to the user, present it not as a technical demo, but a nice presentation of the stickers as their UFC Sticker Pack. At the end, offer some suggestions for new poses or words to use, and tell the user they can download the stickers and use them on Messenger, WhatsApp, IG DMs, etc.116:T6513,Create four personalized fighter stickers using the three supplied images.\n\nInputs\n\n  Input 1: User identity photo\n    Supplies identity, face, skin tone, age, hair, facial hair, accessories.\n\n  Input 2: Exact outfit reference\n    Supplies complete shirt, shorts, gloves with logos, patterns, materials, colors, placement. When creating the reference image, do not give the user shoes. They should not be given shoes.\n\n  Input 3: Typography sheet\n    Transparent image with four cells: top left GOAT, top right LFG, bottom left AND NEW, bottom right UNDISPUTED. This is actual artwork, not inspiration. If uploaded with white background, convert white to transparent and remove, preserving text shape. Do not fill transparent areas with color.\n\nSegmentation background rule (applies everywhere)\n\n  Use ONLY pure flat uniform green #00FF00 (RGB 0, 255, 0) for all generated backgrounds. This green is for segmentation only and will be removed to transparency later. Do not use magenta #FF00FF, pink, purple, or any other background color. No gradient, texture, shadows, or vignette in the background.\n\nText artwork source rule (applies to every generation and edit)\n\n  The only allowed lettering source is the original prepared typography crop image.\n  Every image generation or image edit that shows lettering must have {{word artwork}} attached as an image input.\n  A filename, phrase, written description, or previously generated sticker is never a substitute.\n  Do not redraw, retype, trace, substitute, approximate, or reinterpret the lettering.\n  Keep the lettering in front of the fighter, at its original size and position.\n  This applies to initial creation, corrections, retries, and all follow-up turns.\n\nStage 1: Canonical fighter reference\n\n  Use Input 1 + Input 2 to generate one isolated full-body reference of the person wearing the supplied outfit.\n\n  - Preserve recognizable facial structure, skin tone, age, hair, facial hair, hat/glasses/religious coverings.\n  - Glasses, hats, religious coverings, hearing devices, and any other visible face/head accessories in Input 1 are part of the person's identity. Preserve them in the canonical fighter reference and all four stickers, with the same design, colors, and way they are worn. Do not remove, replace, or redesign them for a pose or expression.\n  - Natural photographic anatomy, no exaggerated muscles, no enlarged head/hands/feet.\n  - Transfer exact shirt, shorts, gloves: cuts, lengths, colors, materials, patterns, logos, placement. Natural folds allowed. Do not simplify or invent marks.\n  - Bare feet unless footwear in Input 2.\n  - Photorealistic, slightly stronger contrast, moody directional shadows. Editorial grade, not illustration.\n  - One person, neutral standing pose, entire hat/hair, both hands, both legs, both feet visible, comfortable margins.\n  - Generate on flat uniform green #00FF00, no shadows or gradient. No border. No magenta. Retain as canonical fighter reference. Do not present to user.\n  - Do not give the user shoes.\n\n  Before proceeding, confirm that the reference retains the face/head accessories visible in Input 1 and that the background is pure #00FF00.\n\nStage 2: Split typography\n\n  Read Input 3 dimensions, crop at horizontal and vertical midpoints into:\n    text_goat (top-left), text_lfg (top-right), text_and_new (bottom-left), text_undisputed (bottom-right).\n\n  Preserve full canvas, placement, alpha. Do not tightly crop lettering. Keep each cell's original dimensions and preserve the text's exact size and coordinates relative to that cell. Top stays top, middle stays middle, bottom stays bottom; horizontal placement also stays unchanged. Do not move, resize, recenter, stretch, or reflow the words. Do not extend the cell canvas. Use these unchanged crops as {{word artwork}} for generation and every subsequent edit. Do not fill transparent areas with green or magenta.\n\nStage 3: Four parallel image edits (border in-generation)\n\n  Run one complete image-edit per cell in parallel. Each task gets: its prepared typography crop as base, the canonical fighter reference, and Input 2 as support. Do not pass only text descriptions.\n\n  Use the same sub-agent prompt below for all four tasks. Substitute only {{word}}, {{word artwork}}, {{pose}}, and {{output file}} using the definitions after the template. Replace {{pose}} with the complete pose description, not a pose number or summary. Resolve {{word artwork}} to the actual prepared image and attach it; its name alone is not an image input.\n\n  Do not paraphrase, shorten, embellish, or add different instructions for different agents. The only per-agent differences are those four substitutions and the corresponding typography crop. Every agent receives the same canonical reference and outfit image.\n\n  Include the same border description and complete all-pixel background-removal instructions in every agent task, including corrections. Define one shared deterministic cleanup routine and make all agents reuse it; do not have each agent independently invent its segmentation algorithm. Keep image generation parallel. If agents cannot share the routine, collect their untouched renders and run that same cleanup routine on all four centrally.\n\n  Follow-up routing\n\n    The follow-up edits and retries rule inside the sub-agent template is mandatory for the parent and every sub-agent on this turn and all follow-up turns. Keep each sticker associated with its original prepared typography crop. Apply the rule to every correction, including edits unrelated to lettering. For follow-up jobs, use its correction template rather than inventing a new prompt. Run independent corrections to affected stickers in parallel where supported; leave unaffected stickers unchanged.\n\nSub-agent prompt template\n\n  Begin sub-agent prompt\n\n    Create the {{word}} sticker and return {{output file}}.\n\n    Segmentation background: Use ONLY pure flat uniform green #00FF00. Do not use magenta #FF00FF or any other color. No gradient, texture, or shadows in the background.\n\n    Text source: {{word artwork}} is the only lettering source and must be attached. Do not retype the words.\n\n    You have three attached images in this order:\n      1. {{word artwork}}: the prepared typogr